    "summary": "Monster The Other One jam, soaring Philo stomp and tight set",
    "blurb": "The show delivers a monster The Other One and a soaring Philo stomp bass solo, with He's Gone building to a climactic finish. Jerry's guitar remains crisp throughout while the rhythm section locks in tight. High‑energy improvisations make this a must‑listen 1972 performance.",
    "review": "October 24, 1972 at the Milwaukee Performing Arts Center finds the Dead firing on all cylinders. The first set rolls through classics like \"Don't Ease Me In\" and \"Sugaree\" before launching into an expansive second half where the band really stretches out. A highlight is the blistering \"The Other One\" sequence – Jerry shreds with razor‑sharp leads while Phil erupts in a near‑solo bass marathon that fans have dubbed the \"Philo stomp.\" The momentum carries through a thunderous \"He's Gone,\" which climaxes the set, and the adjoining jams on \"Playing In The Band\" and \"Truckin'\" keep the energy soaring. Keith’s keyboards add subtle color under the mix, and Bill's drumming provides a solid, propulsive foundation without ever stealing the spotlight. Thanks to Miller’s pristine soundboard capture, every nuance of this high‑energy night shines through, making it one of the standout 1972 performances.",
